Subject: [PATCH] Model the MIPS-I load delay of MFC2/CFC2 and delay-slot loads

On the R3000A a load (LB/LH/LW/LBU/LHU) and a COP2 move (MFC2/CFC2) write
their GPR one instruction late. The CFG emitter modeled that only for
in-block pairs of ordinary loads: MFC2/CFC2 were written immediately, and
a load in a branch delay slot was written before the branch's successor
ran.

Tekken 2 (USA Rev 1) depends on both at once. Its fighter vertex loop
(0x80072890) is software-pipelined: `bgtz t9, 0x800728CC` carries
`mfc2 t0, SZ1` in its delay slot, and the target's first instruction is
`mtc2 t0, VXY0`, which on hardware still reads the vertex word loaded
into t0 by `lw t0, 0(t8)`. Writing t0 early handed VXY0 the depth value
(Y = 0), so every RTPT batch after the first projected a long spike: the
"exploding fighters" in every in-fight frame. The dirty-RAM interpreter
keeps eager writes in game RAM, so PSX_FORCE_INTERP=1 showed the same.

- MFC2/CFC2 join the in-block load-delay pairs (delayed_gpr_dest).
- scan_delay_slot_loads() finds a delayed write in a Branch/J delay slot
  whose in-function successor reads the register first. The slot writes
  psx_dsl_<slot>; the arm into that successor sets psx_dslp_<slot>, and
  the successor block retires the write after its first instruction (or
  drops it when that instruction writes the register). Other arms write
  back before the transfer. The successor skips the (parked) precise
  slice while a write is pending, so the C local never leaves the
  function. Unmodelable shapes (successor outside the function, a branch,
  or a load of the same register) are logged and keep the old writeback.
- Tekken 2: one site (0x80072948 -> 0x800728CC); no in-block MFC2/CFC2
  pairs; the generated diff is that function only.
- test_cop2_delay_slot_load.py (ctest cop2_delay_slot_load): the Tekken
  loop shape plus an in-block `mfc2; addu`; fails 8 checks without this
  change. The OpenBIOS emitter fingerprint does not cover
  code_generator.cpp, so its stamp is unchanged.

